var e={"1.1":{codice:"1.1",titolo:"About me",livello:"chi sono",breve:"Hi! I'm Guglielmo and I study Computer Engineering at Politecnico di Milano. I am from the province of Ravenna, where I attended a science high school in Lugo, and I have lived in Milan since September 2024. My interests lie in applied mathematics: modelling, simulation and numerical computing. I am also interested in artificial intelligence, which I see as a tool in service of these fields.",blocchi:[{t:"p",x:"Since September 2026 I've been doing research at NECSTLab, a research lab at the Politecnico working on computing systems, on federated learning with tree-based models, that is, training a model without moving the data. It's part of LEONARDO, the lab's extracurricular programme, which I've been part of since March 2026. I'm also a consultant at SMC, a company that has worked in heat exchange and fluid storage since 1995: I'm developing its new management system, the software it uses for quotes, orders, delivery notes and invoices."},{t:"p",x:"I joined PoliMi Data Scientists, the Politecnico's student association for data science and AI, in October 2025 as a web developer. Then I joined {{scheda:3.1|MARCS}}, a research project of the association to recognise ships that switch off their transponder, where I work on the route model. Today I'm Head of Projects: I coordinate the student teams of the Projects section and, as Team Project Manager, the Clinical Risk Detector for Physiotherapy team. I'm also a mentee at LeadTheFuture, a mentorship programme for STEM students."},{t:"p",x:"Outside of coursework I spend a lot of time coding on my own: tools I need, ideas I want to try and, now and then, something optimised more than it needed to be. A few things that came out of it: a program that hunts for bargains on Vinted, a Monte Carlo simulation that estimates the probability that a poker hand wins, and a week of meals computed as an optimisation problem. They're all on the projects page. I share my course notes and the tools I use to organise university here on the site, for anyone who wants to take them and adapt them."},{t:"p",x:"Outside my studies: music production, guitar, piano, chess and bouldering."}],approfondimenti:[],en:!0},"1.2":{codice:"1.2",titolo:"CV",livello:"chi sono",breve:"The up-to-date CV, entry by entry, following the layout of the PDF CV: for each experience the organisation and the dates, the role and the location, and bullet points on what was done. The main entries link to their own page.",blocchi:[{t:"h2",x:"Education"},{t:"h3",x:"Politecnico di Milano \xB7 Sep 2024 \u2192 Jul 2027"},{t:"p",x:"*BSc in Computer Engineering \xB7 Milan*"},{t:"ul",x:["Currently in the third year.","Main courses: Mathematical Analysis 1 and 2, Linear Algebra and Geometry, Physics, Computer Science Fundamentals (C), Computer Architecture and Operating Systems, Algorithms and Principles of Computer Science, Probability and Statistics, Software Engineering (Java)."]},{t:"h3",x:"Summer School \u201CData Science & AI for Business\u201D \xB7 14\u201325 Jul 2025"},{t:"p",x:"*POLIMI Graduate School of Management \xB7 Milan*"},{t:"ul",x:["Admitted through an application and a motivation letter; two full-time weeks, taught in English, at the end of the first year.","Programme ranging from data representation to the basics of neural networks, with business cases and presentations by IBM and Deloitte and a visit to Deloitte's offices.","Three-hour hands-on Python lab on data cleaning."]},{t:"h3",x:"Liceo G. Ricci Curbastro \xB7 Sep 2019 \u2192 Jul 2024"},{t:"p",x:"*Scientific high school diploma \xB7 Lugo*"},{t:"h3",x:"Kaplan International Languages \xB7 Jul 2022, Jul 2023"},{t:"p",x:"*Intensive English courses \xB7 Cambridge and Dublin*"},{t:"ul",x:["Two intensive summer courses in an international setting.","Cambridge English B2 First obtained in 2023 with 174/190, within the B2 band (160\u2013179)."]},{t:"h2",x:"Experience"},{t:"h3",x:"SMC \xB7 14 Sep 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*IT Process & Software Consultant \xB7 Italy*"},{t:"p",x:"A company working in heat exchange and fluid storage since 1995."},{t:"ul",x:["Development of the company's new management system, the software for quotes, orders, delivery notes and invoices: from the analysis of existing processes to the implementation of the software.","Requirements gathering and analysis through meetings with users and a functional analysis of the existing system, with every requirement traced back to its source.","Design of a multi-agent development pipeline on Claude Code: ten specialised agents (functional and technical analysis, design, development, code review, verification), each started from an automatically generated rule kit; project memory in an Obsidian vault linking sources, requirements, decisions and rules; a single-source rule base, cited by code and checked by hooks and lint; a Python command-line tool for search and view generation; approval of every change before implementation.","Automated interface testing on a dedicated Windows environment."]},{t:"p",x:"Technologies: C#, .NET, SQL Server, xUnit, Claude Code."},{t:"h3",x:"NECSTLab \xB7 Politecnico di Milano \xB7 Sep 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*Undergraduate Student Researcher \xB7 Milan*"},{t:"p",x:"Research lab at Politecnico di Milano working on computing systems."},{t:"ul",x:["Research on federated learning for tree-based models (XGBoost), that is, training a model without moving the data.","Goal: measuring the actual accuracy gap between the bagging, cyclic and histogram-based methods and assessing whether a middle ground exists."]},{t:"p",x:"Technologies: Python, XGBoost, NVIDIA FLARE, Flower."},{t:"h3",x:"LEONARDO Project \xB7 Mar 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*Student Member \xB7 Milan*"},{t:"p",x:"NECSTLab's interdisciplinary extracurricular programme for students who want to go beyond their courses."},{t:"ul",x:["Programme built on three pillars, research, learning and well-being: engineering education, research activities, development of technical and transversal skills.","A stepped path, from exploring the options to specialising, towards academic research, industry or a venture of one's own."]},{t:"h3",x:"MARCS Research Project \xB7 Jan 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*Researcher \xB7 PoliMi Data Scientists \xB7 Milan*"},{t:"ul",x:["MARCS (Maritime Activity Recognition & Correlation System): recognising vessels that switch off their AIS transponder to fish in prohibited areas; contribution to the route model.","Selection of the study area, the Strait of Sicily, after comparing the candidate areas on about a dozen criteria; for lack of data, testing moved to Danish waters, where AIS data is open.","Development of the AIS data pipeline: cleaning (from 20.7 to 1.4 million rows per day), chunked sorting, resampling and route segmentation; 66 days of data, about 50 GB.","Training optimisation for the model (GeoTrackNet, in TensorFlow): time per training pass reduced from days to minutes."]},{t:"p",x:"Technologies: Python, TensorFlow, pandas, NumPy."},{t:"h2",x:"Leadership and activities"},{t:"h3",x:"LeadTheFuture Mentorship \xB7 Sep 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*Selected Mentee \xB7 Italy*"},{t:"ul",x:["Selected for a non-profit mentorship programme for STEM students, with an acceptance rate below 20%.","A path of at least one year with a dedicated mentor, in the 2026\u201327 class, with access to the programme's community: interview preparation, CV reviews, workshops."]},{t:"h3",x:"PoliMi Data Scientists \xB7 Oct 2025 \u2192 now"},{t:"p",x:"*Student association for data science and AI \xB7 Politecnico di Milano*"},{t:"p",x:"**Head of Projects** \xB7 Aug 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"ul",x:["Coordination of the student teams of the Projects section, from project definition to delivery.","Selection of new section members through structured interviews with a common set of questions.","Involvement in choosing the projects submitted to the annual funding call."]},{t:"p",x:"**Team Project Manager \xB7 Clinical Risk Detector for Physiotherapy** \xB7 Apr 2026 \u2192 now"},{t:"ul",x:["Coordination of the team developing a decision-support tool for physiotherapists: starting from the recorded visit, it suggests whether to treat the patient or refer them to a doctor.","Running the weekly meetings and assigning tasks.","Setting the project's direction and the purpose of the finished tool.","Management of relations with the physiotherapists collaborating on the project and of contacts with physiotherapy practices."]},{t:"p",x:"**Web Developer** \xB7 Oct 2025 \u2192 Aug 2026"},{t:"ul",x:["Development and maintenance of the association's official website."]},{t:"h3",x:"NECSTLab \xD7 Arduino Hackathon \xB7 14\u201315 May 2026"},{t:"p",x:"*SafeNet, hackathon prototype \xB7 Politecnico di Milano*"},{t:"ul",x:["Prototype on Arduino UNO Q, built in two days in a team of three, to support an elderly person living alone: door sensors and a model that flags anomalies in their routine.","Contribution: the schedule model (Isolation Forest), the pitch storyline and narrating the demo."]},{t:"h2",x:"Projects and research"},{t:"h3",x:"Personal explorations \xB7 2025 \u2192 now"},{t:"ul",x:["Personal projects across optimisation, simulation and automation: Equity (Monte Carlo, C and Python), Axioma (mixed-integer linear programming), Physics Simulation (numerical simulation), Vinted Tracker (automation).","Personal tool: the lecture transcriber."]},{t:"h2",x:"Skills and interests"},{t:"p",x:"The skills, each with its evidence, are on the page (dati/sito.js)."},{t:"ul",x:["**Languages**: Italian, native; English B2, with the Cambridge English B2 First (174/190).","**Technical interests**: artificial intelligence, numerical computing, optimisation, simulation, algorithms.","**Interests**: music production, guitar, piano, chess, bouldering."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:4.4|Il CV da studente}}","{{testo:2.1|Ha senso fare un'internship durante la triennale?}}","{{testo:4.1|Candidarsi, ricevere dei no e riprovare}}","{{testo:5.2|Candidarsi a una magistrale all'estero}}","{{testo:12.1|Cosa conviene sapere al primo anno}}"],en:!0},"2.1":{codice:"2.1",titolo:"Federated learning with trees",livello:"ricerca",breve:"Since September 2026 I've been doing research at NECSTLab, a research lab at Politecnico di Milano working on computing systems, on federated learning with tree-based models. The research is part of LEONARDO, NECSTLab's extracurricular programme with engineering education, research activities and technical and transversal skills, which I've been part of since March 2026. Federated learning is a way to train a model without moving the data: the model goes to the data. Supervised by two of the lab's PhD students, I study how a machine learning model made of decision trees can be trained this way. There are three ways to do it, and none of them is fully satisfactory: two are fast but lose accuracy, the third is accurate but very slow. The research sets out to measure how big this gap really is and to understand whether there is a middle way.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"A few participants build a tree together, without pooling their data",dati:[["federato"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The research tools",dati:[["Python"],["XGBoost"],["NVIDIA FLARE","a federated learning library: the histogram-based recipe"],["Flower","another federated learning library, for bagging and cyclic"],["scikit-learn"],["pandas"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The project's code and data are private."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:3.1|Fare ricerca gi\xE0 in triennale}}"],en:!0},"3.1":{codice:"3.1",titolo:"MARCS",livello:"progetto",breve:"MARCS (Maritime Activity Recognition & Correlation System) is a research project run by a team of students at {{scheda:5.3|PoliMi Data Scientists}}, the student association for data science and AI at Politecnico di Milano, and its goal is to recognise ships that switch off their transponder to fish where they shouldn't. The project brings together three ways of looking at the sea: the signals ships use to report where they are, satellite radar images and optical ones. I contribute to developing the route model, which uses the ships' signals, in particular to training and data preparation: the dataset, data loading and the pipeline that prepares the data. The project started from the Strait of Sicily, which remains the best setting for testing; because of complications and a lack of data, the work moved to Danish waters, where the data is open. The project is ongoing: two versions of the route model have been trained, at two different sampling rates (one point of the route every few minutes, with different intervals), but testing on real cases has not been done yet.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"AIS signals, data cleaning and the ship that disappears and reappears far away",dati:[["marcs"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The AIS signal and the parts of the project"},{t:"p",x:"Instead of switching off the AIS signal, someone fishing where they shouldn't can also falsify it. Other team members work on the satellite radar images, which see ships even at night and through clouds, and on the optical ones; another member works on anomaly detection. The route model starts from GeoTrackNet, an already published recurrent network for trajectories."},{t:"h2",x:"Choosing the area"},{t:"p",x:"In the comparison between the areas, besides confirmed cases of illegal fishing and cloud cover, what counted was how often satellites pass over, the type of fishing and traffic, how clear-cut the boundaries of the no-fishing zones are, the laws and the weather."},{t:"vis",tipo:"confronto",didascalia:"The areas considered, and why",dati:[["Northern Adriatic","The European alternative, weighed against Sicily"],["Strait of Sicily","Chosen: heavy, mixed traffic between Europe and Africa"],["Strait of Sicily","More well-defined no-fishing zones, a much-studied area"]]},{t:"h2",x:"Data cleaning and the pipeline"},{t:"p",x:"From the Danish data I kept only the columns the model needs (time, ship, position, speed, course), I also removed rescue vessels, and I picked six windows of days across the year, from the seasonal fishing ban, the period when fishing is suspended, to the summer peak, so that the model wouldn't learn a single season."},{t:"vis",tipo:"schema",didascalia:"The data pipeline, in its final version",dati:[["Download","The daily files, one per day"],["Clean","Only the useful columns, no shore stations, one point every half minute"],["Sort","By ship and by time, in chunks, without filling up memory"],["Resample","One point at fixed intervals, at several different rates, using only observed points: no invented positions"],["Cut","Fixed-length sequences, that is, stretches of a single ship's route; those with gaps that are too long are dropped"],["Split","Training and validation split by ship, so the same ship doesn't end up in both"]]},{t:"p",x:"Reading in chunks and computing the normalisation statistics up front made training fit in memory, with the weights saved at every pass so it could resume. Compiling the training step and preparing the sequences once, on file, brought a training pass down from days to minutes. GeoTrackNet was ported to the new version of TensorFlow by a teammate."},{t:"h2",x:"Project status"},{t:"p",x:"Still to be done: training on all the periods, and a decision on stationary ships, which make up about half the sequences: whether to keep them or remove them."},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["TensorFlow","with TensorFlow Probability"],["GeoTrackNet","the starting model"],["pandas and NumPy"],["Google Colab"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The starting model: [GeoTrackNet, arXiv 1912.00682](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.00682).","The data: [the open AIS data of the Danish Maritime Authority](http://aisdata.ais.dk/)."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.4|Le associazioni studentesche}}","{{testo:11.2|Perch\xE9 fare progetti}}"],en:!0},"3.2":{codice:"3.2",titolo:"SafeNet",livello:"progetto",breve:"SafeNet is the prototype the three of us built during a two-day hackathon in May 2026, on a board that had just come out, the Arduino UNO Q. The hackathon was organised by NECSTLab, a research lab at Politecnico di Milano working on computing systems, together with Arduino, which provided the boards. The prototype is a home system that notices when something is wrong for an elderly person living alone: two sensors on the door tell whether she is going out or coming back, a model learns her schedule, and the word \u201Caiuto\u201D (help) sets off the alarm. After the hackathon the project remained a prototype.",blocchi:[{t:"h2",x:"What the prototype had to monitor"},{t:"p",x:"The starting point was an elderly person at home and three things to keep an eye on: falls, recognised from their sound; habits of going out and coming back; and a simple way to ask for help. And everything had to fit on a single board, in the middle of a two- or three-room flat."},{t:"h2",x:"How the door recognises an exit or an entry"},{t:"vis",tipo:"porta-safenet",didascalia:"The door, the times, the missing return: SafeNet in four chapters",dati:[["porta"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"schema",didascalia:"The UNO Q: two brains on the same board, and the bridge between them",dati:[["The microcontroller","Reads the sensors, the badge, the buzzer and the LEDs, to the millisecond"],["The bridge","Passes events between the microcontroller and the Linux computer"],["The Linux computer","Python, the model, the dashboard"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The presentation and the demo"},{t:"p",x:"We pitched the prototype twice: first for the selection, then at the final event, in front of representatives of Qualcomm and Arduino, with a live demo."},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Arduino UNO Q","microcontroller and Linux on the same board"],["Arduino App Lab","the board's development environment: word recognition and the dashboard"],["Python"],["scikit-learn","the Isolation Forest"],["Edge Impulse","a platform for training models that run on boards: the fall classifier"],["Motion sensor and magnetic contact"],["RFID badge, buzzer and LEDs"],["Telegram","the alerts"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The code is in the hackathon repository, which belongs to a teammate: the link will come once the repository is public."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.3|Gli hackathon}}"],en:!0},"3.3":{codice:"3.3",titolo:"Clinical Risk Detector for Physiotherapy",livello:"progetto",breve:"At {{scheda:5.3|PoliMi Data Scientists}}, the student association for data science and AI at Politecnico di Milano, I coordinate, as project manager, a team of students who are working, together with some physiotherapists, on Clinical Risk Detector for Physiotherapy, a decision-support tool. More and more patients go to a physiotherapist without seeing a doctor first, and the physiotherapist has to work out whether the case is theirs or should be sent elsewhere. The finished tool will have to listen to the visit, answer a list of questions and suggest a course of action, showing why, while the decision stays with the physiotherapist.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"giro-visita",didascalia:"How a visit goes, in five chapters, on three made-up cases",dati:[["visita"]]},{t:"h2",x:"Warning signs"},{t:"p",x:"In the profession, warning signs are called flags, with different colours for different levels of risk. Red flags indicate high risk: for example, pain that wakes you up at night, fever, weight lost for no reason, or a trauma with signs of a fracture. In private practice, which is the growing sector, the physiotherapist is often alone, whereas in a hospital there is always a doctor nearby."},{t:"h2",x:"The decision-support tool"},{t:"p",x:"The tool is a clinical decision-support system, for what is known as *screening for referral*: working out who should be referred to another professional. Besides suggesting a course of action, it flags what doesn't add up between what was said during the visit and the questions that matter."},{t:"h2",x:"The project manager role"},{t:"p",x:"As project manager I steer the project's direction, organise the weekly meetings, keep in touch with the physiotherapists who collaborate with us and reach out to physiotherapy practices, which are the ones the finished tool will have to help. The team is small and changes, because students have exams, internships and jobs, so part of the work is also finding someone to come in when someone leaves."},{t:"ul",x:["The projects page of PoliMi Data Scientists: https://www.polimidatascientists.it/projects"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.4|Le associazioni studentesche}}"],en:!0},"4.1":{codice:"4.1",titolo:"Axioma",livello:"esplorazione",breve:"Axioma is a program that plans a week of meals and draws up the shopping list. You pick the foods you like and switch on the rules to follow; the program treats the week as an optimisation problem: among the quantities that respect the rules, it looks for the best ones. It lets you shop for a few days knowing you'll eat well, without weighing everything, a need I had as a student living away from home. It's a small project, but it was one of the first in which what I was studying in Computer Engineering became a tool I use. The app is online and the code is on GitHub.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"Pick the foods and the rules: the week gets solved and the shopping list comes out at the end",dati:[["axioma"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The starting sheet on Notion"},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The Notion sheet where it all started (August 2025)",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-1-axioma-notion-alimenti.webp","The list of foods: for each one the category, how much was needed over five days (which was also the shopping list) and the values per 100 grams"],["immagini/schede/4-1-axioma-notion-obiettivi.webp","The week's targets, the deviation rule (how far you can stray from the targets) and how the calories are split between protein, carbohydrates and fat: the three percentages add up to 100"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The model: variables, constraints and objective"},{t:"p",x:"The quantities are chosen by a solver, a program built specifically for optimisation problems. The model is written with PuLP, a Python library, and solved by CBC, the solver that comes with PuLP."},{t:"vis",tipo:"solver-axioma",didascalia:"How the program reasons, step by step: from trial and error by hand to the solved week",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"Foods, units and axioms"},{t:"p",x:"The name comes from logic, where a theory starts from a few rules taken as true, the axioms, and everything else follows from them. I rethought the diet the way you build a theory: the foods arranged as a tree, from general categories down to single foods; the units, that is the meal, the day and the week; and axioms that say when a meal is valid, how often a food can come back, when the week is balanced. In the app every axiom is a rule you switch on with a tick: how to build the meals, how often meat, meal prep. Each food also has one to five stars, to say how much you like it."},{t:"h2",x:"The app and the tools"},{t:"p",x:"The interface is built with Streamlit. Behind it sit the model, the rules and the solver; between the two parts there are data contracts, which fix what is asked of the solver and what it answers."},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The app: the foods, the plan and the shopping list",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-1-axioma-alimenti.webp","The foods: which ones are active, the minimum and maximum portion, the stars"],["immagini/schede/4-1-axioma-piano.webp","A five-day plan at the lowest cost"],["immagini/schede/4-1-axioma-spesa.webp","The shopping list for the same plan, with the estimated cost"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["PuLP","to write the model"],["CBC","the solver"],["Streamlit","the interface"],["pandas"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The app: https://dietmaker-gsdlqnfz4rb4dcuq4fzftx.streamlit.app/","The code: https://github.com/Willy00blu/Diet_Maker (Diet Maker is the name the project had before Axioma, and it stayed in the addresses of the app and the code)"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.2|I progetti personali}}"],en:!0},"4.10":{codice:"4.10",titolo:"The robotic chessboard",livello:"esplorazione personale",breve:"The robotic chessboard is a personal design experiment of mine, beyond software: a chessboard for playing against the computer, which moves the opponent's pieces by itself, dragging them with a magnet that slides under the board. I didn't build it: it exists as a 3D model, as a simulation and as a long list of problems, some solved and some not. What I spent the most time on is working out where the person playing has moved.",blocchi:[{t:"h2",x:"The chessboards on the market"},{t:"p",x:"Chessboards that move their own pieces are sold commercially, but they cost several hundred euros. I wanted to try building one myself, with a cheaper approach than the small robots some of them put inside every piece."},{t:"vis",tipo:"partita-3d",didascalia:"A game against the computer, seen from the inside",dati:[["e4"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The mechanism: a bridge and an electromagnet under the board"},{t:"p",x:"Under the playing surface there's a bridge, that is a beam that crosses the box from one side to the other as in 3D printers, and along the bridge runs a carriage with an electromagnet: by moving the bridge and the carriage, the magnet can reach any point under the squares. Every piece has a small magnet in its base, so when the electromagnet's coil switches on, the piece stays coupled through the board and follows the carriage, and when it switches off, the piece stops right there. The carriage never lifts, and from above you only see the piece sliding: so rather than an arm, it's a bridge working from underneath. The rules and the game engine aren't in the machine but in a phone app, connected to the chessboard, which tells it which pieces to move."},{t:"vis",tipo:"scacchiera-3d",didascalia:"The machine opened up: it can be rotated, viewed from below and made to move a knight",dati:[["b1","c3"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The starting requirements"},{t:"ul",x:["**Magnet**: close to the board.","**Captured pieces**: at the sides, not in a drawer.","**Footprint**: square frame, box as small and low as possible (the versions with the shell reached almost sixty centimetres for a twenty-four-centimetre chessboard: too much).","**Mechanics**: motors and circuits inside, with a structure for the mechanics and a shell on top.","**Documentation**: an assembly manual with figures, procedures and checks, before any purchase."]},{t:"h2",x:"The technical problems I ran into"},{t:"p",x:"As I went on, problems came up that I hadn't foreseen at the start. These are the ones that changed the project."},{t:"vis",tipo:"carte",didascalia:"The problems, one by one",dati:[["The magnet and the thickness of the board","The magnet's force collapses with distance, so the board has to be just a few millimetres thick; but a board that thin, forty centimetres wide, bends. Hence the glass board, which bends much less than plastic, and the supports on all four sides"],["The piece that won't let go","Even with the coil off, the iron core keeps attracting the piece's magnet, which stays stuck: it takes a brief pulse of reverse current, and a short wait before moving off again"],["The knight and the lanes","A travelling piece moves along the border between two squares, and there it brushes against its neighbours: already on the knight's first move the bases were touching. The bases have to be less than half a square wide"],["Pieces that push each other away","Two nearby magnets facing the same way push each other off, and up close the push beats friction. In the end I enclosed the piece's magnet in an iron cup"],["The friction of the base","With a rough base no magnet is enough, because the harder you pull, the more the piece presses down on the board: the material under the piece matters as much as the magnet"],["Where captured pieces go","They needed a place, and with two rows per side a promotion (the pawn that reaches the far end and becomes another piece, taken from the captured ones) left a piece trapped: so a single row per side"],["The footprint","The box shrank by more than ten centimetres per side and got a good deal lower, until everything fit"],["The belt that didn't touch","In one of the drawings the belt only grazed the pulley by half a tooth: built like that, the machine wouldn't have moved"],["The wrong magnets","The ones I received were ferrite, not neodymium: I noticed with a ruler and a kitchen scale, by watching at what distance they repelled each other on a glass table"]]},{t:"h2",x:"How the chessboard recognises the move"},{t:"p",x:"The machine always knows where it has put its own pieces, because it moved them itself. What it doesn't know is what the person on the other side does: which piece they picked up, where they put it down, whether they knocked into another one in the meantime. Without this information it can't reply, and if it gets it wrong even once the whole game goes out of sync. There were quite a few ideas: in the tool below you can try them one by one, with what was good about each and the reason why, on its own, it wasn't enough."},{t:"vis",tipo:"riconosci-mossa",didascalia:"One move, and how each idea would see it",dati:[["app","I tell it, in the app","I type the move on the phone, or I let the machine move my pieces too","No sensors, and the state is always consistent","One lapse, a move made and not typed in, and the chessboard no longer knows where the pieces are","the choice for the prototype"],["memoria","The machine remembers","The program keeps track of every piece's position, with no sensors at all","It removes dozens of sensors, the wiring and hours of assembly","It knows where the pieces should be, not where they really are","not enough"],["caselle","A sensor under every square","Sixty-four magnetic sensors, one per square, which sense the magnet in the piece's base","It's the best-known approach, and the pieces already have the magnet","Lots of wiring; the cheapest sensors, from under the board, don't sense enough, and placed closer they spoil the coupling","discarded"],["testa","Sensors on the head","A few field sensors around the magnet, travelling with it","Nothing under the board, and while a piece is being moved you can see whether it really follows","To read the whole chessboard the head has to visit every square, and that takes too long","useful while moving pieces"],["fissi","A few fixed sensors and magnetic fingerprints","A handful of stationary sensors under the board: the field before and the field after, compared with the fingerprint each piece would leave on each square","It reads the move without moving anything, and can tell even if the piece is a little off-centre","The signal is tiny inside the Earth's magnetic field, and moving metal parts disturb it: it can only be read with the machine still","where I had got to"],["ponte","A row of sensors on the bridge","Eight sensors in a row on the moving beam, one per column, passing under the squares","Much closer to the pieces, so a much stronger signal, and fewer components","To read, the bridge has to make a pass","to be decided after the measurements"],["vibrazione","Vibrations","Three small piezoelectric sensors under the edge of the glass, which pick up the tap of a piece being put down","Noticing that a piece has been put down is very easy, and costs almost nothing","Working out where from the sound is hard, so it only serves to wake up the magnetic reading","provided for"],["bobina","The coil as a sensor","The magnet's own coil: a piece on top changes the way it responds","No extra sensor needed, and it confirms that the piece is coupled","It only sees what's directly above it","to be measured"]]},{t:"h2",x:"How far the experiment got"},{t:"p",x:"The experiment went as far as it can go without building anything. The next step was a real test bench, with a magnetometer costing a few euros (a sensor that measures the magnetic field, like the ones considered for recognising the move) and an afternoon of measurements, to see whether the simulation's numbers hold up outside the computer too. I didn't buy the mechanics, and of the parts only the magnets arrived. Since the end of August the project has been on hold, and the test bench is the first thing I would start again from."},{t:"h2",x:"The 3D model and the images from the ideation phase"},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The 3D model and the in-browser simulator it was tested on",dati:[["immagini/schede/scacchiera-cad-v3-con-pezzi.webp","The latest version of the 3D model: the CoreXY mechanics, the bridge, the magnetic head and the pieces, with the shell and the glass hidden"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-cad-v3-meccanica.webp","The same machine, mechanics only"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-modello-dall-alto.webp","The version of the interactive model above, seen from above without the board: the frame, the bridge with the magnet, the motors in the corners and the electronics at the back"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-modello-da-sotto.webp","The same model seen from below, without the bottom"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-banco-virtuale.webp","The virtual test bench: the machine's parameters and the knight travelling along the lanes between the squares"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-gemello-digitale.webp","The digital twin: a real game, where each move turns into the transfers the machine would have to make"]]},{t:"p",x:"The images below come from the first days and were meant to give me an idea of how it might turn out."},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"Generated with AI during ideation: they aren't photos, and the measurements aren't reliable",dati:[["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-estetica-cornice-quadrata.webp","The 24-centimetre chessboard, with the square frame and the captured pieces on both sides: the look I wanted"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-variante-legno.webp","A wood and aluminium variant"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-variante-nera.webp","A black, closed variant"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-esploso-sbagliato.webp","The exploded view with the magnet too low, far from the board: the discarded one"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-esploso-corretto.webp","The corrected exploded view, with the carriage just under the board"],["immagini/schede/scacchiera-ia-ponte-con-sensori.webp","The idea of the bridge with the row of sensors, one per column"]]}],approfondimenti:[],en:!0},"4.2":{codice:"4.2",titolo:"Vinted Tracker",livello:"esplorazione",breve:"Vinted Tracker is a program for personal use, built to find deals on Vinted, the second-hand marketplace: listings priced far below what is typical for the same product. It started from a simple task and turned into a much harder one. The first version just sent an alert when a new listing came up for the saved searches. The last one brings together the listings for the same product, written any old way and in seven languages, to work out which price is really a deal, and deals are searched for on demand: the listings that ended up in mixed groups went down from about one in four to fewer than two in a hundred. The project is finished, and the code of the last version is on GitHub.",blocchi:[{t:"h2",x:"The typical price and product groups"},{t:"p",x:'In the definition of a deal, the hard part is "the same product". For example, to know what a 27-inch 4K Samsung monitor usually costs, you first have to find all the listings about that monitor, and only that one: not the 28-inch ones, not the LG monitors, not the HDMI cables whose title says "Samsung monitor". Almost all the work went there, into making clean groups, with consistent prices.'},{t:"p",x:"A group's typical price is the median, the price in the middle: half the listings cost less, half cost more. Around the median, the prices of the same used product spread out in a bell shape, and a deal is a price much lower down, outside the bell."},{t:"h2",x:"The first version: alerts on new listings"},{t:"p",x:"The first version was a desktop app: each search with its own keywords, minimum and maximum price, brands, words to exclude and how many minutes between checks. When a new listing comes up an alert arrives, and if the Wi-Fi drops, it runs a full scan again once the connection is back."},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The web interface of the last version (the first was a desktop app)",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-2-vinted-ricerche.webp","The searches: for each one the filters (price, words, brand, exclusions), how many listings it has seen, the market median and the alert history"],["immagini/schede/4-2-vinted-occasioni.webp","The deals: the listings whose price is furthest from their group's median, with how far the comparison can be trusted"],["immagini/schede/4-2-vinted-categorie.webp","A whole category: how many listings are active and how many presumed sold (explained further down), the price distribution, the groups of similar products, the price drops"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The stages of the pipeline"},{t:"p",x:"In the last version every listing goes through a series of stages, always in the same order, which together make up the pipeline: from cleaning the title to the comparison with its group's price. In the simulation below the stages are the real ones from the code, but the seven listings are made up: the ones from the example above (the 27-inch 4K Samsung in three different listings, the 28-inch, the LG and the HDMI cable) plus an unbranded monitor. The first chapter shows them with title and price. In the last two stages, to show how the prices are spread, more made-up listings are added to the group."},{t:"vis",tipo:"pipeline-vinted",didascalia:"Seven listings through the pipeline: from cleaning the titles to the deal",dati:[["annunci"]]},{t:"h2",x:"How the quality of the groups is measured"},{t:"p",x:"A wrong group doesn't show at a glance: you have to go looking for it. For example, in a Nintendo Switch group, consoles and games had ended up together, and every game looked like a discounted console: the same problem as the cable in the simulation."},{t:"p",x:"To find them I built a yardstick, that is a score that says how much of the catalogue ends up in mixed groups, how much is left on its own and how consistent the prices are. With that yardstick each variant of the pipeline can be tested in a few seconds, and the best ones are confirmed on the whole database, hundreds of thousands of listings. Many ideas were tried and dropped. The one that made the difference is the mixture check, that is stage 4 of the simulation (groups with two products), the last step before the price."},{t:"h2",x:"Titles, descriptions and photos"},{t:"p",x:"Groups are built on the titles. I tried the listings' descriptions and dropped them, because they talk almost only about condition and shipping. Photos come in later, and only where the prices don't add up, to split a group in which two different items live side by side: used everywhere, they would risk splitting apart the very listings that belong together. In any case the images are never saved, and all that is kept on the photo side is a vector, that is a list of numbers: as with the titles in stage 3, it turns the photo into a point in a space where similar things sit close together."},{t:"h2",x:"Sold listings and removed listings"},{t:"p",x:"In my view the typical price shouldn't come only from what people ask, but also from what things actually sell for. That's why a continuous check goes back over the active listings. If the page is still there but the buy button isn't, the item is presumably sold, at the last price seen; if the page no longer exists, it has been removed and doesn't count as a sale. Price drops are recorded too."},{t:"h2",x:"The last version's interface and the tools"},{t:"p",x:"The interface is a web app that runs on the computer and opens in the browser. The searches, deals and categories tabs are in the photos above; the fourth is for exploring a category with a live search on Vinted, looking at each price against its group's median. Clicking a group shows its price bands, the distribution and the sales over time."},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["vinted_scraper","the open-source library for reading Vinted"],["SQLite","the listings database"],["e5","the model that turns titles into vectors, in every language"],["HDBSCAN","the algorithm that forms the groups"],["CLIP","the model that turns photos into vectors"],["NiceGUI","the interface"],["Telegram","the first version's alerts"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The code: https://github.com/Willy00blu/vinted-tracker"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.2|I progetti personali}}"],en:!0},"4.3":{codice:"4.3",titolo:"Equity",livello:"esplorazione",breve:"Equity is a program that estimates how likely a poker hand, in the Texas Hold'em variant, is to beat the hands the opponent might hold; in poker this probability is called, precisely, equity. It's an exploration to understand the Monte Carlo method on a concrete case: instead of counting every combination, the program fills in the rest of the table at random tens of thousands of times and counts how it ends. Poker here serves only as a probability model, because it looks like a game of luck while every situation has a precise number. To get to that number, the cards already seen and the weight of each group of hands both matter, and you need to know how far the estimate can be trusted. The engine is written in C, the window and the charts in Python.",blocchi:[{t:"h2",x:"What you know at the table and what you don't"},{t:"p",x:"At the table nobody sees everything. In Texas Hold'em each player has two face-down cards, and five shared, face-up cards come out in the middle; the first three, which come out together, are called the flop. Each player makes the best five-card combination out of their own two and the five shared ones: from the weakest, high card, to the strongest, straight flush, going through pair, two pair and the others; when the combination is the same, the highest cards decide, the tie-breakers. So you know your own two cards and the face-up ones in the middle; you don't know the opponent's cards, but you can know what kind of hands they play, for example only high pairs and a few strong aces."},{t:"p",x:`In the table below the cards are written with their initials (A ace, K king, Q queen, J jack): AA is a pair of aces, AQ an ace and a queen. "Suited" means the two cards are of the same suit: both hearts, diamonds, clubs or spades. Next to each of the opponent's hands is the number of its combinations; when there are two numbers, the crossed-out one is the total and the other says how many are still possible after the cards already seen.`},{t:"vis",tipo:"tavolo-poker",didascalia:"The table: in each simulated hand the opponent is dealt, at random, one of the hands they play, among those still possible",dati:[["tavolo"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The Monte Carlo method"},{t:"p",x:"The method is explained in the scene below, which starts from \u03C0 and ends up at Equity's table."},{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"The same method in the simplest case: random points in a square estimate \u03C0",dati:[["montecarlo"]]},{t:"h2",x:"Cards already seen and conditional probability"},{t:"p",x:"The cards you can see change the opponent's possible hands: that's conditional probability. If you hold an ace, the opponent can have at most the other three, and all their combinations that use that card disappear. The same goes for the cards on the table. At first the program gave a small probability even to a hand that, given the face-up cards, couldn't exist; that's why impossible combinations are removed before simulating."},{t:"h2",x:"The weighted average over groups of hands"},{t:"p",x:"The opponent's hands are split into groups, and each group has a different number of combinations. A pair of kings can be made in 6 ways, suited ace-queen in 4, offsuit in 12. To get a single number, the probability against the whole set, averaging the groups isn't enough: you need a weighted average."},{t:"vis",tipo:"formula",didascalia:"The probability of winning against the whole set of hands",dati:[["P = \\frac{\\sum_i p_i \\, c_i}{\\sum_i c_i}","Each group of hands counts as much as the combinations it really has"],["p_i","the probability of winning against group i, estimated by the simulation"],["c_i","the combinations of group i that are still possible, leaving out those with cards already seen"]]},{t:"h2",x:"How precise the estimate is"},{t:"p",x:"The typical deviation from the \u03C0 scene (the fourth chapter) also applies to the simulated hands: with tens of thousands of simulations the estimate now moves by only a few tenths of a point."},{t:"vis",tipo:"formula",didascalia:"The typical deviation of the estimate",dati:[["\\sigma \\approx \\sqrt{\\frac{p\\,(1-p)}{N}}","More trials, less uncertainty, but ever more slowly"],["p","the true probability of winning"],["N","how many hands are simulated"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The app's charts, drawn with the real engine",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-3-equity-grafico-en.webp","One hand against six groups of hands: the probability against each and the overall, weighted one"],["immagini/schede/4-3-equity-convergenza-en.webp","Ace-king against a pair of queens: with few hands the estimate jumps around, then it settles"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The engine in C"},{t:"p",x:"Simulating tens of thousands of hands for each group means evaluating millions of hands. That's why the core is in C, compiled as a library and called from Python. What makes it fast is a simple idea: each hand becomes a single number, with the category, that is the type of combination, in the most significant digits and, in the lower ones, the cards that break ties. So, to know who wins, comparing two numbers is enough. The simulation runs in a separate thread, that is a line of execution separate from the window's, so the window doesn't freeze while the engine works."},{t:"p",x:"The evaluator, the part that decides which hand wins, is simplified: in some rare ties it compares fewer cards than it should. For understanding the method that's fine; for counting those cases to the decimal place, it isn't."},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["C","the simulation engine"],["Python","with ctypes, to call the C code"],["Tkinter","the window"],["matplotlib","the charts"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The code: https://github.com/Willy00blu/Equity"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.2|I progetti personali}}"],en:!0},"4.5":{codice:"4.5",titolo:"Physics Simulation",livello:"esplorazione",breve:`Physics Simulation is a program that animates four mechanics phenomena: the elastic collision, the inelastic collision, the "real" collision with a coefficient of restitution (a number between 0 and 1 that says how much the bodies bounce off each other) and an explosion into fragments, with gravity. You choose masses, velocities and coefficient and watch what happens. It's a small exploration: I wanted to learn object-oriented programming with something I could see moving, and for that I took the physics of the first year of Computer Engineering. It's written in Python and uses Tkinter, the graphics library already included in Python, so there's nothing to install; the code is on GitHub.`,blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"Two bodies on a track: change the masses and the coefficient and watch momentum and energy",dati:[["urti"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The collision formulas"},{t:"p",x:"Two quantities matter in the formulas: momentum, that is mass times velocity, summed over the two bodies, which is always conserved in a collision; and kinetic energy, the energy of motion."},{t:"vis",tipo:"formula",didascalia:"The elastic collision: momentum and energy are conserved",dati:[["v_1' = \\frac{(m_1 - m_2)\\,v_1 + 2\\,m_2\\,v_2}{m_1 + m_2}","And the symmetric one for the second body"],["m_1, m_2","the two masses"],["v_1, v_2","the velocities before the collision"],["v_1'","the velocity of the first body afterwards"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"formula",didascalia:"The inelastic collision: the two bodies stick together",dati:[["v' = \\frac{m_1 v_1 + m_2 v_2}{m_1 + m_2}","Momentum is conserved, energy is not"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"formula",didascalia:"The real collision: how much relative velocity is left after the collision",dati:[["v_2' - v_1' = -\\,e\\,(v_2 - v_1)","With e = 1 it's elastic, with e = 0 inelastic"],["e","the coefficient of restitution, between 0 and 1"]]},{t:"p",x:"The explosion, the fourth simulation, has no collision formula: the last chapter of the animation further down tells its story."},{t:"h2",x:"The animation loop"},{t:"vis",tipo:"fotogramma-urti",didascalia:"From the formulas to the program, in five chapters",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"The app and the tools"},{t:"p",x:"The three parts of the program are in the fourth chapter of the animation; below are the real screenshots."},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"The app",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-5-physics-home.webp","The menu: the four simulations"],["immagini/schede/4-5-physics-urto.webp","The real collision, halfway through the simulation"],["immagini/schede/4-5-physics-esplosione.webp","The explosion: the fragments in flight above the floor"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["Tkinter","the window and the animation"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The code: https://github.com/Willy00blu/Physics_simulator (in the repository the project is called Physics Simulator)"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.2|I progetti personali}}"],en:!0},"4.6":{codice:"4.6",titolo:"The lecture transcriber",livello:"esplorazione",breve:"The lecture transcriber is a program that takes the recording of a lecture I attend at Politecnico di Milano all the way to a tidy note in my vault, the archive of notes I keep in Obsidian. It transcribes the audio on the Mac, has Claude, Anthropic's artificial intelligence, write the note, matches the slides, captures the blackboard before it gets wiped and redraws the figures; then it logs the lecture and the tasks in Notion, a notes and database app. I use it every week.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"From recording to note: transcription on the Mac, the note, the slides and figures, the Obsidian shelf",dati:[["nastro"]]},{t:"h2",x:"Notes without listening to the lecture again"},{t:"p",x:"The way I use it, the pipeline isn't a way to skip lectures: in class you listen and try to understand, and the machine writes the note. Recordings come in from two places: lecture recordings, downloaded from WeBeep, and recordings from Pocket, the recorder described under Recommended apps, which connects directly to the pipeline through its MCP server (a channel through which other programs can read its recordings): on the app's Pocket page you choose which ones to bring in. Every evening at eight, an automatic run processes whatever is still waiting, if I haven't done it already, and prepares a document for each of the next day's lectures."},{t:"h2",x:"Time with and without the pipeline"},{t:"vis",tipo:"due-tempi",didascalia:"One lecture: watching all of it, or running it through the pipeline",dati:[["@nota","Medians over 26 lectures recorded between 16 September and 9 October 2026, from the pipeline's logs: a lecture lasts 94 minutes, the pipeline takes 31 (from 14 to 73) from transcription to saved note."],["Watching the whole recording","94","done"],["Running it through the pipeline","31","note ready"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The note and the uncertain points"},{t:"p",x:"Of the rules for the note, the one I care about most concerns the uncertain points: the question that takes the place of a gap is cleared up later with the slides or with a classmate."},{t:"h2",x:"The blackboard and the figures"},{t:"p",x:"The program finds the blackboard frame by looking at how much of the board is written on over time, ignoring the lecturer walking in front of it. The redrawn figures are plots, phase portraits (drawings of the trajectories of a dynamical system) and circuits; each course has an index of its figures, so a later lecture reuses the ones already made."},{t:"vis",tipo:"immagine",didascalia:"A note produced by the pipeline, in my Obsidian vault: an Electronics Fundamentals lecture on the CMOS inverter",dati:[["immagini/schede/4-6-nota-grafico.webp","The graph of the static characteristic, drawn by the pipeline as SVG; the note says which numbers are an example chosen for it"],["immagini/schede/4-6-nota-proprieta.webp","The same section: the property in a box, with its formula, and the matched course slides, gathered in a callout that opens"],["immagini/schede/4-6-nota-tabella.webp","The table summarising the five operating ranges of the two transistors"]]},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["Whisper","transcription, on the Mac"],["Claude","the note"],["ffmpeg, PyMuPDF, OpenCV","audio, slides and blackboard"],["matplotlib and schemdraw","the figures"],["Notion API"],["Obsidian"],["Electron","the app with the list of lectures"]]},{t:"ul",x:["What you need to use it is explained below, in the transcriber package."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:8.3|Gli strumenti per stare al passo}}","{{testo:8.1|Quando ha senso seguire le lezioni}}","{{testo:10.4|Costruire con gli agenti}}"],en:!0},"4.7":{codice:"4.7",titolo:"My first open source contribution",livello:"esplorazione",breve:"In mid-October 2025, during Hacktoberfest, I installed git and sent my first three pull requests to other people's open source projects. One was merged: the one to the first-contributions tutorial, which is there to teach you the full GitHub workflow. The two with my password generator, a small command-line Python program, were closed without being merged.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"The first push: fork, branch, commit, and GitHub asking for a token instead of the password",dati:[["github"]]},{t:"h2",x:"The first-contributions tutorial"},{t:"p",x:"I had never built a project, and October was Hacktoberfest month, so I started with open source. first-contributions is a repository made for learning the full workflow: fork, clone, branch, commit, push and pull request."},{t:"h2",x:"The password generator for 100LinesOfPythonCode"},{t:"p",x:"The next day I wrote a command-line password generator for 100LinesOfPythonCode, a Hacktoberfest repository that collects programs of under 100 lines, each in a single file."},{t:"p",x:"The options (length, how many passwords, symbols, letters, digits) go through `argparse`, and `random` picks the characters. The first bug was the case where no character type is chosen, because then the set to draw from is empty. For passwords the right module would have been `secrets`; I used `random` anyway."},{t:"h2",x:"The requests, day by day"},{t:"vis",tipo:"tappe",didascalia:"Two days in October 2025",dati:[["14 October","git and the tutorial","Homebrew, git, the first push with the token. The pull request to first-contributions is merged the same day."],["14 October","A proposal","On AlgoVisualizer (React, Vite, Tailwind) I propose redesigning the speed selector. It's an issue, closed on 1 November."],["15 October","The password generator","Two pull requests to 100LinesOfPythonCode: the first closed in under a minute, the second closed without being merged in February 2026."]]},{t:"h2",x:"How the requests ended"},{t:"p",x:"The tutorial pull request adds your own name to the list of contributors and contains no code. The two forks are no longer on my GitHub account, but the copies are still on my computer."},{t:"h2",x:"Tools and links"},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"What I used",dati:[["git"],["GitHub","fork, branch, pull request, personal access token"],["Homebrew"],["Python","argparse and random"],["ChatGPT","explanations of the steps"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The first-contributions tutorial, on GitHub: the project, not my pull request.","The Hacktoberfest website **[da verificare]**."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.2|Perch\xE9 fare progetti}}","{{testo:11.1|Mettersi alla prova in triennale}}"],en:!0},"5.1":{codice:"5.1",titolo:"SMC",livello:"esperienza",breve:"SMC is a company that has worked in heat exchange and fluid storage since 1995.",blocchi:[{t:"p",x:"**Page in preparation.**"}],approfondimenti:[],en:!0},"5.2":{codice:"5.2",titolo:"NECSTLab and LEONARDO",livello:"esperienza",breve:"NECSTLab is a Politecnico di Milano lab that works on computing systems, and I'm part of it in two roles. Since March 2026 I have been part of LEONARDO, its programme for students, and since September 2026 I have been doing research in the lab on federated learning with tree-based models, supervised by the PhD students. Both are ongoing; the research has a page of its own.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"racconto-necstlab",didascalia:"The lab and LEONARDO, in five chapters",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"The two roles in the lab"},{t:"vis",tipo:"carte",didascalia:"The two CV entries",dati:[["Student Member, LEONARDO","Since March 2026. The lab's interdisciplinary programme: engineering education, research activities, technical and transversal skills."],["Undergraduate Student Researcher, NECSTLab","Since September 2026. Two PhD students supervise the research, with a meeting every week."]]},{t:"h2",x:"Research on federated learning"},{t:"p",x:"The research page: {{scheda:2.1|Federated learning with trees}}."},{t:"vis",tipo:"tecnologie",didascalia:"The tools",dati:[["Python"],["XGBoost"],["NVIDIA FLARE and Flower"],["scikit-learn"]]},{t:"ul",x:["The LEONARDO programme: https://leonardo.polimi.it/"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:3.1|Fare ricerca gi\xE0 in triennale}}","{{testo:12.2|LinkedIn da studente}}"],en:!0},"5.3":{codice:"5.3",titolo:"PoliMi Data Scientists",livello:"esperienza",breve:"PoliMi Data Scientists is the Politecnico di Milano student association for artificial intelligence, machine learning and data science. I joined in October 2025 to look after the website; over time the roles grew to four, often held at the same time, and since August 2026 I have been Head of Projects, in the section that oversees the projects.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"scena",didascalia:"One step at a time: the roles in PoliMi Data Scientists",dati:[["scala"]]},{t:"h2",x:"What the association does and how it oversees projects"},{t:"p",x:"Since 2019 it has been on the Politecnico's register of student associations. It organises hands-on workshops (machine learning, language models, data engineering), extracurricular team projects that last a few months and often start together with a lab or a company, meetings with professors and professionals, company visits, competitions and a reading group. It is split into teams: board, events, tech, communication, projects, people, finance, startup relations and a section dedicated to quantum computing."},{t:"p",x:"Every project overseen by the Projects section has a project manager and a person from the section for the organisational side, that is, rooms, external contacts and deadlines."},{t:"ul",x:["The association's website: https://www.polimidatascientists.it/"]},{t:"h2",x:"The roles in the association"},{t:"p",x:"Two of the roles, shown in the scene above, have their own page: {{scheda:3.1|MARCS}} and {{scheda:3.3|Clinical Risk Detector for Physiotherapy}}."}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:11.4|Le associazioni studentesche}}"],en:!0},"5.4":{codice:"5.4",titolo:"LeadTheFuture",livello:"esperienza",breve:"LeadTheFuture is a non-profit organisation that pairs students in STEM subjects (science, technology, engineering and mathematics) with a mentor who already works in the field they're interested in, in research or in industry. The path lasts at least one year, at no cost to participants. Since September 2026 I've been part of it as a mentee, in the 2026\u201327 class, the one running from September 2026 into 2027, and I have a mentor I talk to one-on-one.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"racconto-ltf",didascalia:"LeadTheFuture, in three chapters",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"How the programme works"},{t:"p",x:"You're admitted if there is a match between your goals and the expertise of the available mentors, and the mentor is chosen based on the preferences given in the application: engineering, mathematics, physics, computer science, biology, biotechnology, neuroscience or medicine, towards research or towards industry. The relationship includes a call every three months, and the cycle goes from an initial meeting, to a progress check, to the final results."},{t:"p",x:"Around the relationship with the mentor there is a community, which you join once admitted: university guidance, study groups to prepare for interviews, CV reviews, an opportunities board, workshops and meet-ups to get to know each other."},{t:"ul",x:["The programme: https://leadthefuture.tech/"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:6.2|Avere un mentore durante l'universit\xE0}}","{{testo:4.1|Candidarsi, ricevere dei no e riprovare}}"],en:!0},"5.5":{codice:"5.5",titolo:"Summer School Data Science & AI for Business",livello:"esperienza",breve:`"Data Science & AI for Business" is a summer school of the Graduate School of Management of Politecnico di Milano: two weeks in Milan on how data and artificial intelligence feed into a company's decisions.`,blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"racconto-summer",didascalia:"The summer school, in three chapters",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"How the summer school is organised"},{t:"p",x:"It is one of the four summer schools of the Graduate School of Management: it is held in person, on the campus in Navigli, a district of Milan, and it is aimed mainly at students finishing their bachelor's degree, international students and people early in their careers. It alternates lectures, workshops, hands-on projects and company visits, with a few sessions on CVs and careers."},{t:"ul",x:["The summer schools of the Graduate School of Management: https://www.gsom.polimi.it/en/programmes/summer-school/"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:6.3|Le summer school: valgono quello che costano?}}"],en:!0},"5.6":{codice:"5.6",titolo:"Kaplan and the Cambridge B2",livello:"esperienza",breve:"During high school I took two intensive English courses with Kaplan, in Cambridge in 2022 and in Dublin in 2023, and in 2023 I obtained the Cambridge English B2 First, with 174 out of 190. After the B2 came the lessons for the IELTS Academic, with a tutor.",blocchi:[{t:"vis",tipo:"racconto-kaplan",didascalia:"Kaplan and the B2, in four chapters",dati:[]},{t:"h2",x:"The IELTS lessons and the links"},{t:"p",x:"The IELTS lessons, the notes and what I've understood about how to tackle the different parts of the exam are shared on the [English](inglese/) page."},{t:"ul",x:["Kaplan International: https://www.kaplaninternational.com/","The Cambridge B2 First: https://www.cambridgeenglish.org/exams-and-tests/qualifications/first/","IELTS: https://ielts.org/"]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:7.1|Quale certificazione d'inglese scegliere}}"],en:!0},"6.2":{codice:"6.2",titolo:"The transcriber package",livello:"universit\xE0",breve:"The package is the lecture transcriber in a downloadable version: the pipeline, which takes a recorded lecture all the way to a note in Obsidian, and its app.",blocchi:[{t:"ul",x:["**Software**: open source","**Status**: package in preparation","**Requirements**: Mac with an Apple chip (Apple Silicon), Claude subscription"]},{t:"h2",x:"What's in the package"},{t:"vis",tipo:"confronto",didascalia:"In the package",dati:[["Included","The pipeline, in Python"],["Included","The app with the list of lectures and the settings, a modified version of WeBeep Sync"],["Included","An example configuration file"],["Not included","The lecture recordings"],["Not included","The transcripts and the notes"],["Not included","My data and my Notion settings"]]},{t:"p",x:"The lecture recordings aren't included and won't be, because they are course material: everyone adds their own by hand, as the first chapter of the animation shows."},{t:"h2",x:"The starting projects and the licences"},{t:"p",x:"The pipeline and its app come from two different programs by different authors. Their names are almost the same because both work on WeBeep, the Politecnico's course platform:"},{t:"ul",x:["**WebeepSync**: the tool presented by two members of PoliMi Data Scientists, which downloads lecture recordings from WeBeep, transcribes them and makes notes from them with an AI. The pipeline's starting idea comes from here; the code is almost entirely new.","**WeBeep Sync**: an open source program by another author, which downloads course materials from WeBeep to the computer. The pipeline's app is a fork of it, that is, a copy of the code developed independently."]},{t:"p",x:"Some ideas also come from two public repositories (code projects open to everyone) credited in the README, the package's instructions file: picking the frames where something new appears, matching pages to speech, the vocabulary drawn from the lecture notes, and the link format for recordings made with WebEx (a video conferencing service) and published on WeBeep."},{t:"p",x:"The licences still need sorting out: the fork declares GPL-3.0 in its licence file and MIT in `package.json`, and has to be aligned on GPL-3.0, WeBeep Sync's licence. The pipeline doesn't have a licence file yet."},{t:"h2",x:"Requirements"},{t:"ul",x:["**System and hardware**: macOS on Apple Silicon (arm64), required by mlx-whisper for transcription.","**Software**: Python and ffmpeg; optional OCR, with macOS's own or with tesseract.","**Note writing**: Claude Code, with a Claude subscription; alternatively gemini or ollama, both supported by the README.","**Applications**: Obsidian for the notes; Notion for the list of courses and activities used to classify each recording."]},{t:"h2",x:"Costs"},{t:"ul",x:["**Transcription**: local, on the Mac, at no cost.","**Notes**: Claude Code on the subscription, not the pay-as-you-go API; each lecture uses part of the subscription.","**Usage**: a system prompt of about 2,000 tokens; the review pass with the large model off by default."]}],approfondimenti:["{{testo:8.3|Gli strumenti per stare al passo}}"],en:!0}};export{e as SCHEDE};
